## Starting FCFF and explicit forecasts

FY2025 reference calculation: after-tax interest = 31.481 × (1 − 0.271) = 22.949649. Starting FCFF = −155.755 + 22.949649 − 172.375 = −305.180351, rounded to −305.180. Sources: K, cash-flow statement p. 68, interest table p. 46, tax table p. 47 / Note 12 p. 92. The model does not multiply this negative starting value by growth rates.

Every future component below is an estimate adopted for this lab on September 10, 2026, not company guidance. Formula: FCFF = operating cash flow + after-tax interest − gross capital expenditures.

| Year | OCF estimate | After-tax interest estimate | Gross capex estimate | FCFF estimate | Scenario reasoning and supporting locator |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| 2026 | 50 | 23 | 575 | -502 | Operating recovery with heavy investment; K, Item 7, Magnetics p. 50 and Liquidity p. 51 |
| 2027 | 150 | 23 | 600 | -427 | Assume better utilization but peak construction spending; K, Item 7 pp. 41–42; Note 16, Contract Balances p. 97 |
| 2028 | 225 | 23 | 450 | -202 | Assume only limited initial 10X cash contribution; K, Item 7, Downstream Manufacturing p. 42 |
| 2029 | 325 | 23 | 250 | 98 | Assume construction spending falls and cash conversion improves; K, Item 7 p. 51; Note 3, Offtake Agreement p. 79 |
| 2030 | 400 | 23 | 175 | 248 | Successful-ramp scenario; K, Item 7 pp. 41–42, 51; Note 3 p. 79 |

Disclosure anchors: GM magnet sales expected in 2026; 10X commissioning in 2028; 2026 capital costs of 500–600 net of awards; increasing working-capital needs (K, Item 7 pp. 42, 50–51). DoW price support and capacity-linked EBITDA provisions are in Note 3 pp. 78–79. These disclosures support scenario timing, not the exact forecast amounts.

OCF budgets for 2026–2030 and annual capex budgets after 2026 are unresolved as company guidance; the explicit estimates above fill those modeling gaps. The 2026 gross-capex estimate assumes 550 net spending plus 25 award receipts; the 25 is an estimate. The 23 interest addback rounds the starting-year calculation and is held constant without a future financing or cash-tax schedule. OCF estimates assume continued working-capital consumption and avoid counting prepaid deliveries as new receipts. Financing proceeds are not FCFF. The guarantee is not consolidated FCFF.

## WACC and terminal growth reconciliation

| WACC component | Value | Unit | Date | Source / classification |
|---|---:|---|---|---|
| Risk-free rate | 4.83 | % annually | September 9, 2026 | H, nominal 10-year row; observed |
| Beta | 1.95 | Unitless | September 10, 2026 | M, Beta (5Y); provider estimate |
| Equity risk premium | 5.00 | % annually | Lab assumption, adopted September 10, 2026 | Professor's methodology supplied by user |
| Representative note coupon | 3.00 | % annually | December 31, 2025 terms | K, Note 10 p. 87, 2030 notes; proxy for debt cost |
| Effective tax rate | 27.1 | % | FY2025 | K, Note 12 p. 92; reported |
| Debt fair value for WACC | 2378.651 | USD millions | December 31, 2025 | K, Note 19 p. 103; 82.449 + 2172.782 + 98.081 + 25.339 |
| Equity market capitalization | 9140 | USD millions | September 10, 2026 close | M, Total Valuation; rounded observed value |

Cost of equity = 4.83% + 1.95 × 5% = 14.58%. After-tax debt cost = 3% × (1 − 27.1%) = 2.187%. Debt weight = 2378.651 / (2378.651 + 9140) = 20.6504%; equity weight = 79.3496%. WACC = 79.3496% × 14.58% + 20.6504% × 2.187% = 12.0208%, rounded to the adopted 12.02%.

This preserves the previously selected classroom estimate. Historical fair-value debt is used for WACC, while carrying debt is used for the balance-sheet bridge. Coupon versus market yield, different dates, convertible-option value, and preferred-stock treatment limit consistency. A same-date comprehensive capital-cost estimate remains unresolved; no new value is invented.

Terminal growth = approximately 2% real GDP growth + 2% inflation = 4%, based on F's longer-run medians. PCE inflation is a proxy for GDP-deflator inflation, so 4% is an estimate rather than a published nominal-GDP forecast. It reflects the economy, not MP's or rare-earth market growth. 4% < 12.02%; spread = 8.02 percentage points.

## Bridge conventions and limitations

Cash includes liquid investments and excludes restricted cash. Its treatment assumes operating valuation excludes investment income and includes future operating/capital spending; an exact minimum operating-cash deduction remains unresolved. Do not deduct forecast capex from opening cash again.

Debt includes both convertible issues, Samarium borrowing, and equipment notes. Equipment borrowing is in other liabilities (K, Note 10 p. 89). The debt entry is gross carrying debt, not net debt or total liabilities. Operating lease expense stays in operating cash flow; operating lease liabilities are not separately capitalized in this simplified model.

The EPS denominator follows the professor's instruction. Antidilutive instruments are excluded from that denominator (K, Note 20 p. 104). Preferred stock is a separate claim (K, Balance Sheets p. 64). Its valuation and conversion treatment remain unresolved in the simplified EV + cash − debt bridge. Consequently the printed equity value is the lab bridge result, not a comprehensive allocation to common equity. No unsupported preferred-value adjustment is inserted.

